1
00:00:00,631 --> 00:00:03,133
By the year 2030,

2
00:00:03,133 --> 00:00:09,373
nearly half of all the young people walking this earth will be African.

3
00:00:09,373 --> 00:00:15,212
And being able to read is the scale upon which so many other skills depend.

4
00:00:15,212 --> 00:00:18,215
Yet, more than 80 percent

5
00:00:18,215 --> 00:00:20,617
of 10-year-old children in sub-Saharan Africa

6
00:00:20,617 --> 00:00:23,487
cannot read a simple text with understanding.

7
00:00:23,487 --> 00:00:24,955
Not because they're not smart,

8
00:00:24,955 --> 00:00:27,057
not because they didn't pitch up for school,

9
00:00:27,057 --> 00:00:30,594
but because the system is failing them.

10
00:00:30,594 --> 00:00:33,864
The go-to solution for this learning crisis has always been the same:

11
00:00:33,864 --> 00:00:35,899
build more schools, build more classrooms,

12
00:00:35,899 --> 00:00:38,268
hire more teachers, train more teachers.

13
00:00:38,268 --> 00:00:40,437
Sounds reasonable.

14
00:00:40,437 --> 00:00:46,143
But here’s the uncomfortable truth no one really wants to say out loud:

15
00:00:46,143 --> 00:00:49,346
It's actually never going to work.

16
00:00:49,346 --> 00:00:52,049
To meet ideal student-to-teacher ratios,

17
00:00:52,049 --> 00:00:56,987
sub-Saharan Africa needs 15 million more teachers

18
00:00:56,987 --> 00:01:03,126
at a staggering cost of almost $40 billion a year.

19
00:01:03,126 --> 00:01:05,362
It's just nuts.

20
00:01:05,362 --> 00:01:08,332
So why are we still holding on to a playbook

21
00:01:08,332 --> 00:01:12,235
that cannot possibly deliver at the cost and urgency

22
00:01:12,235 --> 00:01:14,671
and scale required?

23
00:01:14,671 --> 00:01:17,541
Well, it's because we have a weird bias.

24
00:01:17,541 --> 00:01:21,612
Not that kind of weird, but a WEIRD bias --

25
00:01:21,612 --> 00:01:25,616
the idea, the assumption that solutions that work in countries

26
00:01:25,616 --> 00:01:30,387
that are Western, educated, industrialized, rich and democratic

27
00:01:30,387 --> 00:01:33,724
should somehow work everywhere else in the world.

28
00:01:33,724 --> 00:01:38,562
(Applause)

29
00:01:38,562 --> 00:01:42,633
The assumption that WEIRD countries are the standard,

30
00:01:42,633 --> 00:01:44,568
"the way the world works,"

31
00:01:44,568 --> 00:01:46,436
and somehow with more inputs,

32
00:01:46,436 --> 00:01:50,240
African schools should eventually look like WEIRD ones.

33
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:52,542
Let's pause right there,

34
00:01:52,542 --> 00:01:55,245
because that assumption is at the heart of the problem.

35
00:01:55,245 --> 00:01:59,016
And God knows so many others.

36
00:01:59,016 --> 00:02:01,251
We shouldn't have to frame our world like that.

37
00:02:01,251 --> 00:02:03,820
And I certainly have outgrown the belief

38
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:06,289
that African schools should fit a borrowed model.

39
00:02:06,289 --> 00:02:09,459
Our goal should be to build something brilliant

40
00:02:09,459 --> 00:02:13,997
that actually reflects the reality that African children are living in today.

41
00:02:13,997 --> 00:02:18,068
At the organization I lead, it’s called “Imagine Worldwide,”

42
00:02:18,068 --> 00:02:20,637
we are doing something different.

43
00:02:20,637 --> 00:02:26,076
Children like Chisomo, who you will hear about shortly,

44
00:02:26,076 --> 00:02:29,513
are now able to experience something else.

45
00:02:29,513 --> 00:02:34,317
Chisomo is a typical seven-year-old girl living in rural Malawi

46
00:02:34,317 --> 00:02:37,587
with her younger sister and parents who are farmers.

47
00:02:37,587 --> 00:02:39,222
It's a pretty happy existence,

48
00:02:39,222 --> 00:02:41,525
even without iPhones and ketchup.

49
00:02:41,525 --> 00:02:42,793
(Laughter)

50
00:02:42,793 --> 00:02:46,630
Every morning, she walks through the green maize fields

51
00:02:46,630 --> 00:02:50,600
under the glory of the African sun.

52
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:54,071
But at school, she shares a class with a hundred other kids,

53
00:02:54,071 --> 00:02:57,574
sometimes 200 -- if a teacher was absent.

54
00:02:57,574 --> 00:03:00,777
She can barely see the book that the teacher was holding up

55
00:03:00,777 --> 00:03:04,114
in an overcrowded, dimly lit room.

56
00:03:04,114 --> 00:03:07,117
She rarely gets a chance to ask a question, share an answer,

57
00:03:07,117 --> 00:03:10,854
get feedback -- and feedback is everything.

58
00:03:10,854 --> 00:03:12,856
I remember when I tutored high-school math,

59
00:03:12,856 --> 00:03:16,026
some kids didn’t even get their marked tests back --

60
00:03:16,026 --> 00:03:17,861
just a score.

61
00:03:17,861 --> 00:03:21,298
So they never knew where they went wrong

62
00:03:21,298 --> 00:03:23,667
or right, for that matter.

63
00:03:23,667 --> 00:03:25,502
There was no feedback loop,

64
00:03:25,502 --> 00:03:29,306
just this vacuum of uncertainty that makes it impossible

65
00:03:29,306 --> 00:03:32,809
to get better at anything.

66
00:03:32,809 --> 00:03:35,812
And [at] Imagine, we are giving children like Chisomo

67
00:03:35,812 --> 00:03:41,418
up to an hour a day of high-impact learning on a tablet.

68
00:03:41,418 --> 00:03:44,955
I know, I know, I can feel the parents in the room cringing.

69
00:03:44,955 --> 00:03:47,758
We are so allergic to the idea of screens,

70
00:03:47,758 --> 00:03:49,259
they are bad for mental health,

71
00:03:49,259 --> 00:03:50,894
hurting attention spans.

72
00:03:50,894 --> 00:03:54,064
Really not good for your mental and emotional development.

73
00:03:54,064 --> 00:03:56,867
I get that, and that's all true.

74
00:03:56,867 --> 00:03:59,269
In a few contexts.

75
00:03:59,269 --> 00:04:03,073
And this is why context is so important.

76
00:04:03,073 --> 00:04:06,877
Chisomo is not living the WEIRD childhood

77
00:04:06,877 --> 00:04:10,080
that so many of us are trying to protect our children from.

78
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:13,150
Her reality is not too much tech.

79
00:04:13,150 --> 00:04:18,922
It's more closer to none at all, at school or at home.

80
00:04:18,922 --> 00:04:20,724
The solution that we are delivering

81
00:04:20,724 --> 00:04:24,327
is really designed to tackle some of the hardest things

82
00:04:24,327 --> 00:04:27,364
that EdTech has not been able to deliver in Africa.

83
00:04:27,364 --> 00:04:28,765
And I know that many of you

84
00:04:28,765 --> 00:04:33,503
are very skeptical about tech in poor countries.

85
00:04:33,503 --> 00:04:38,074
Our solution is tackling those issues head on.

86
00:04:38,074 --> 00:04:41,044
All those prior failures that were too hardware-focused

87
00:04:41,044 --> 00:04:43,980
relied on power and connectivity.

88
00:04:43,980 --> 00:04:46,082
The ones that were cultural misfits,

89
00:04:46,082 --> 00:04:47,951
lacking in sound pedagogy,

90
00:04:47,951 --> 00:04:49,886
working outside government systems,

91
00:04:49,886 --> 00:04:53,256
or just plain too expensive to run.

92
00:04:53,256 --> 00:04:58,829
All of those, we have figured out a model that works.

93
00:04:58,829 --> 00:05:00,197
Ready to hear it?

94
00:05:00,197 --> 00:05:04,234
(Cheers and applause)

95
00:05:04,234 --> 00:05:07,170
Specially designed tablets that are solar-charged

96
00:05:07,170 --> 00:05:09,506
are used throughout the school day,

97
00:05:09,506 --> 00:05:12,108
and they're designed with a battery that can carry over

98
00:05:12,108 --> 00:05:14,644
and they can be rotated throughout classes.

99
00:05:14,644 --> 00:05:16,913
The content is contextually relevant

100
00:05:16,913 --> 00:05:20,417
and delivered in local languages like Swahili and Chichewa,

101
00:05:20,417 --> 00:05:24,788
including verbal instruction via the headphones.

102
00:05:24,788 --> 00:05:27,190
And it works entirely offline.

103
00:05:27,190 --> 00:05:29,960
No internet, no power, no problem.

104
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:33,096
It's not a replacement for teachers, but an extra helping hand.

105
00:05:33,096 --> 00:05:36,366
The kids only spend about an hour of their day on it anyway.

106
00:05:36,366 --> 00:05:37,734
But in that hour,

107
00:05:37,734 --> 00:05:40,503
the tablet does something incredible:

108
00:05:40,503 --> 00:05:44,441
It adapts to each child's level and pace,

109
00:05:44,441 --> 00:05:46,409
guiding even first-time users,

110
00:05:46,409 --> 00:05:51,314
children who are not yet able to read and have never seen a device,

111
00:05:51,314 --> 00:05:53,183
from their very first tap,

112
00:05:53,183 --> 00:05:58,855
all the way to literacy and numeracy -- without adult intervention.

113
00:05:58,855 --> 00:06:00,857
Imagine what that means

114
00:06:00,857 --> 00:06:05,462
in a classroom where individual attention is almost impossible.

115
00:06:05,462 --> 00:06:10,233
Each child suddenly gets something incredibly rare:

116
00:06:10,233 --> 00:06:14,437
instruction that responds to them.

117
00:06:14,437 --> 00:06:17,540
The child who was struggling can slow down and get more practice.

118
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:21,511
The child who is ready to keep going can rush ahead.

119
00:06:21,511 --> 00:06:26,549
Imagine in a classroom where 20 kids are each on their own tablets, or 200 --

120
00:06:26,549 --> 00:06:30,387
there is no loss in learning quality.

121
00:06:30,387 --> 00:06:33,423
This is all achieved without having to retrain

122
00:06:33,423 --> 00:06:37,193
or restructure the entire education system.

123
00:06:37,193 --> 00:06:40,664
What we're seeing here is greater access to learning

124
00:06:40,664 --> 00:06:43,733
and better learning by the minute.

125
00:06:43,733 --> 00:06:46,569
This is unheard of in education.

126
00:06:46,569 --> 00:06:48,838
97 percent of teachers in Malawi

127
00:06:48,838 --> 00:06:51,541
have confirmed that the tablet program

128
00:06:51,541 --> 00:06:55,645
makes teaching more enjoyable.

129
00:06:55,645 --> 00:07:00,216
(Applause)

130
00:07:00,216 --> 00:07:04,988
And the results speak for themselves.

131
00:07:04,988 --> 00:07:07,724
Nine randomized controlled trials

132
00:07:07,724 --> 00:07:12,062
across four countries in three languages over the past decade

133
00:07:12,062 --> 00:07:14,698
have proven that time-bound,

134
00:07:14,698 --> 00:07:18,802
fit-for-context digital learning embedded in public school systems

135
00:07:18,802 --> 00:07:22,939
is the most scalable way to accelerate literacy

136
00:07:22,939 --> 00:07:26,710
and numeracy outcomes for millions of African children.

137
00:07:26,710 --> 00:07:28,745
We are seeing learning gains

138
00:07:28,745 --> 00:07:31,648
that rank among the top education interventions

139
00:07:31,648 --> 00:07:35,485
anywhere in the world.

140
00:07:35,485 --> 00:07:40,657
(Cheers and applause)

141
00:07:40,657 --> 00:07:44,327
Three years ago, we were already doubling the number of children

142
00:07:44,327 --> 00:07:47,664
that become literate and numerate by their fourth year in school.

143
00:07:47,664 --> 00:07:51,067
Now we're on track to triple that.

144
00:07:51,067 --> 00:07:53,203
(Applause and cheers)

145
00:07:53,203 --> 00:07:56,006
I think so, too.

146
00:07:56,006 --> 00:07:57,607
Beyond test scores,

147
00:07:57,607 --> 00:08:01,344
we're seeing more children pitch up and stay in school.

148
00:08:01,344 --> 00:08:05,215
Girls catch up and keep pace with boys.

149
00:08:05,215 --> 00:08:06,850
And the most important thing,

150
00:08:06,850 --> 00:08:12,255
more children are falling in love with learning.

151
00:08:12,255 --> 00:08:14,524
Imagine takes a system- strengthening approach,

152
00:08:14,524 --> 00:08:17,560
working in partnership with government from day one

153
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:21,064
to embed the program within public education systems,

154
00:08:21,064 --> 00:08:25,668
ensuring a pathway for scale and sustainability.

155
00:08:25,668 --> 00:08:27,003
And through that process,

156
00:08:27,003 --> 00:08:30,206
we're able to create road maps for others to replicate

157
00:08:30,206 --> 00:08:32,909
in more countries in the future.

158
00:08:32,909 --> 00:08:39,582
The best news is this testimony from this innocent girl where she says,

159
00:08:39,582 --> 00:08:44,487
"I like the tablet because when I make a mistake,

160
00:08:44,487 --> 00:08:47,424
it doesn't move on to the next student.

161
00:08:47,424 --> 00:08:53,129
It asks me to try again, and then I can get it right.”

162
00:08:53,129 --> 00:08:57,567
Finally, a school experience where children experience success

163
00:08:57,567 --> 00:09:01,237
and the dignity of real learning.

164
00:09:01,237 --> 00:09:05,809
This year, Imagine will reach one million children.

165
00:09:05,809 --> 00:09:11,614
(Cheers and applause)

166
00:09:11,614 --> 00:09:15,652
And by 2030, thanks to the generosity of this community,

167
00:09:15,652 --> 00:09:19,689
we aim to serve over four million children a year

168
00:09:19,689 --> 00:09:23,426
across Malawi, Sierra Leone and Tanzania

169
00:09:23,426 --> 00:09:26,429
and in the process, create a pathway

170
00:09:26,429 --> 00:09:28,698
for many, many others to follow.

171
00:09:28,698 --> 00:09:33,670
This, this right here is how you turn a tide.

172
00:09:33,670 --> 00:09:35,438
With a model that can deliver

173
00:09:35,438 --> 00:09:39,976
at the scale, quality, cost and urgency required.

174
00:09:39,976 --> 00:09:43,413
Imagine a future where foundational learning

175
00:09:43,413 --> 00:09:46,249
that is of high quality is democratized,

176
00:09:46,249 --> 00:09:49,319
and the majority of every country's children

177
00:09:49,319 --> 00:09:51,187
can learn to read

178
00:09:51,187 --> 00:09:54,757
so that they too can read to learn

179
00:09:54,757 --> 00:09:59,362
and secure a future in skilled work.

180
00:09:59,362 --> 00:10:00,597
We don't need to wait.

181
00:10:00,597 --> 00:10:02,298
We simply need to act and now.

182
00:10:02,298 --> 00:10:04,834
That future is already knocking,

183
00:10:04,834 --> 00:10:09,272
sounding the name of every African child.

184
00:10:09,272 --> 00:10:13,176
(Swahili, Chichewa) Thank you.

185
00:10:13,176 --> 00:10:17,176
(Cheers and applause)
